msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/plots.rst:10
msgid "Spyder main window showing execution for the Plots pane"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/plots.rst:13
msgid "The figures shown in the Plots pane are those associated with the currently active :guilabel:`Console` tab; if you switch consoles, the list of plots displayed (or none at all, if a new console) will change accordingly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/plots.rst:15
msgid "Spyder's plots pane and console showing switching consoles"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/plots.rst:24
msgid "Toolbar options"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/plots.rst:26
msgid "The toolbar at the top of the :guilabel:`Plots` pane provides a number of useful features that allow you to interact with your figures."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/plots.rst:28
msgid "For example, you can cycle sequentially through the plot list with the forward and back arrows."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/plots.rst:30
msgid "Spyder main window showing plot navigation using arrows."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/plots.rst:35
msgid "The :guilabel:`-`, :guilabel:`+` and :guilabel:`Fit plot to pane size` buttons and the associated percentage field allows you to control zoom level."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/plots.rst:37
msgid "Spyder main window showing plots zoom"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/plots.rst:40
msgid "You can save one or all the plots in the pane to image file(s) by clicking the respective :guilabel:`Save plot as` and :guilabel:`Save all plots` buttons in the toolbar. Plots are rendered and saved as PNG (raster) by default, but SVG (vector) can be selected as an option under :menuselection:`Preferences --> IPython Console --> Plotting --> Inline backend --> Format`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/plots.rst:43
msgid "Spyder plots pane showing saving a plot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/plots.rst:46
msgid "Additionally, if you want to use a figure in another document, you can click the :guilabel:`Copy plot to clipboard as image` button and paste your plot wherever you want, such as a word processor."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/plots.rst:48
msgid "Spyder Plots pane showing pasting plot in LibreOffice."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/plots.rst:51
msgid "Finally, you can use the :guilabel:`Remove plot` and :guilabel:`Remove all plots` buttons in the toolbar to clear figures from the list."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/plots.rst:53
msgid "Spyder plots pane showing removing a plot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/plots.rst:64
msgid "The options (\"hamburger\") menu in the top right of the :guilabel:`Plots` pane offers several ways to customize how your figures are displayed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/plots.rst:70
msgid "Mute inline plotting"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/plots.rst:72
msgid "The :guilabel:`Mute inline plotting` option is enabled by default, preventing your plots from also appearing in the :guilabel:`IPython Console`. If you deactivate this option, figures will display in both the :guilabel:`Plots` pane and the console."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/plots.rst:75
msgid "Spyder's plots pane and console showing plots in the console"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/plots.rst:82
msgid "Show plot outline"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/plots.rst:84
msgid "The :guilabel:`Show plot outline` option, off by default, shows a thin stroke surrounding the area of the figure area, which will also appear in the exported images."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/plots.rst:86
msgid "Spyder's plots pane showing a plot's outline"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/plots.rst:93
msgid "Maximum number of plots"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/plots.rst:95
msgid "The :guilabel:`Set maximum number of plots` option, set to ``100`` by default, controls how many recent plots will be accessible via the :guilabel:`Plots` pane. A higher number means you can browse more recent plots, but will use more memory and potentially slow Spyder down substantially if you generate a very high number of plots at once."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/profiler.rst:5
msgid "Profiler"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/profiler.rst:7
msgid "The **Profiler** pane recursively determines the run time and number of calls for every function and method invoked in a file, breaking down each procedure into its smallest individual units. This allows you to identify the bottlenecks in your code, points you toward the exact lines most critical for optimization, and measures the performance delta after followup changes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/profiler.rst:10
#: ../../panes/profiler.rst:90
msgid "Spyder Profiler pane, displaying a list of functions and their execution time"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/profiler.rst:19
msgid "Running the Profiler"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/profiler.rst:21
msgid "You can run profiling for the current :ref:`panes-editor` file, cell, selection or line by clicking the corresponding item in the :guilabel:`Run` menu (i.e. :guilabel:`Profile file`, :guilabel:`Profile cell`, etc), or the respective icon in Spyder's main toolbar."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/profiler.rst:23
#: ../../panes/profiler.rst:53
msgid "Spyder Profiler pane, showing running profiler from menu bar"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/profiler.rst:26
msgid "To cancel an in-progress run and see the results so far, click the :guilabel:`Stop` button on the Profiler pane toolbar. If profiling fails, such as due to a bug in your code, the error output will be shown in the :ref:`panes-console`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/profiler.rst:29
msgid "Results are broken down by function/method/statement, with each sub-element listed hierarchically under the top-level item that called them. To expand or collapse all items by one level of the call stack, click the :guilabel:`+` or :guilabel:`-` buttons in the left of the pane toolbar."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/profiler.rst:32
msgid "Spyder Profiler pane, showing dropdown arrows and buttons for expanding and collapsing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/profiler.rst:35
msgid "Right-click an item in the Profiler and select :guilabel:`Go to definition` to be taken to the file and line in the :ref:`panes-editor` where it was called."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/profiler.rst:37
msgid "Spyder Profiler pane, showing opening a file when clicking on its analysis"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/profiler.rst:40
msgid "You can display all functions regardless of level in the call stack, sorted by :guilabel:`Local Time` (the time spent inside just that function), using the :guilabel:`Show items with large local time` toolbar button."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/profiler.rst:42
msgid "To filter calls to third-party packages outside your own code, use the :guilabel:`Hide calls to external libraries` button."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/profiler.rst:44
msgid "Right-click any function to show every other function that calls or is called by it. You can restore the default view with the :guilabel:`Show functions or modules called by the root item` toolbar button."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/profiler.rst:47
msgid "To find a specific function, click the :guilabel:`Search` button and type its name into the field that is displayed at the bottom of the pane."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/profiler.rst:49
msgid "Finally, you can save the data for a given run to disk as a file with the ``.prof`` extension using the :guilabel:`Save profiling data` button. This can be loaded to compare with a previous run of the same file using the :guilabel:`Load data` button. To remove the loaded data, click the :guilabel:`Clear comparison` button."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/profiler.rst:62
msgid "Interpreting the results"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/profiler.rst:64
msgid "Results are broken down by function/method/statement, with each sub-element listed hierarchically under the top-level item that called them. :guilabel:`Total Time` is that taken by the specified item and every function \"underneath\" (*i.e.* called by) it, while :guilabel:`Local Time` only counts the time spent in the particular callable object's own scope. The :guilabel:`Calls` column displays the total number of times the specified object was called at that level inside its parent calling function (or within the ``__main__`` scope, if a top-level object). Finally, the numbers in the :guilabel:`Diff` columns for each of the three appear if a comparison is loaded, and indicate the deltas between each measurement, with red (+) numbers meaning the current version is slower than the previous comparison, and green (-) numbers meaning it is faster."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/profiler.rst:69
msgid "Profiler with a comparison loaded, displaying the time deltas between two runs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/profiler.rst:72
msgid "For example, suppose you ran the Profiler on a file calling a function ``sleep_wrapper()`` which took a total of 10 ms to run, 9 ms of which was spent in the ``time.sleep()`` function it called in turn. Therefore, if ``time.sleep()`` called nothing else itself, its :guilabel:`Total Time` and :guilabel:`Local Time` would both be identical, at 9 ms. Meanwhile, :guilabel:`Total Time` for ``sleep_wrapper()`` would be 10 ms, but :guilabel:`Local Time` only 1 ms as the rest was spent inside the ``time.sleep()`` function it called."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/profiler.rst:82
msgid "Line profiler plugin"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/profiler.rst:84
msgid "There is an additional plugin that you can install to enable other types of profiling in Spyder. Spyder Line Profiler allows you to benchmark each line of your code individually. To learn more, visit the `spyder-line-profiler git repository <spyder-line-profiler git repository_>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/projects.rst:6
msgid "Projects"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/projects.rst:8
msgid "Spyder allows you to associate a given directory with a **Project**, which automatically saves and restores the files you have open in the :ref:`panes-editor` from the last time you opened that project. With the :guilabel:`Projects` pane, you can browse all your project's files, regardless of your current working directory or :ref:`panes-files` location."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/projects.rst:11
msgid "Spyder showing Project Explorer and projects menu"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/projects.rst:14
msgid "In addition, your project's root folder is used to set your working directory, and automatically added to the ``PYTHONPATH``, so you can easily import and work with any modules and packages you create inside of it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/projects.rst:18
msgid "Projects are completely optional and not imposed on users. All of Spyder's functionality (code completion, session saving, File Explorer, working directory, etc) is available without creating a project."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/projects.rst:27
msgid "Creating a Project"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/projects.rst:29
msgid "To create a project, click the :guilabel:`New Project` entry in the :guilabel:`Projects` menu, choose whether you'd like to associate a project with an existing directory or make a new one, and enter the project's directory name (if a new directory) and path."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/projects.rst:31
msgid "Spyder showing opening a new project"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/projects.rst:40
msgid "Using the Projects Pane"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/projects.rst:42
msgid "Once a project is opened, the :guilabel:`Project` pane is shown, presenting a tree view of the current project's files and directories. It allows you to perform all the same :ref:`operations <panes-files-operations>` as Spyder's :ref:`panes-files` pane."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/projects.rst:45
msgid "Spyder Project Explorer, displaying a directory tree of project files"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/projects.rst:55
msgid "Working with version control"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/projects.rst:57
msgid "The :guilabel:`Project` pane has basic integration with the `Git <Git_>`__ distributed version control system, just like :ref:`in the Files pane <panes-files-vcs>`. You can commit or browse a file, directory or the entire repository via the respective commands in the context menu."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/projects.rst:62
msgid "To use this functionality, the project must be located in a Git repository and the ``git`` and ``gitk`` commands must be on the system path."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/pylint.rst:5
msgid "Code Analysis"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/pylint.rst:7
msgid "The **Code Analysis** pane detects style issues, bad practices, potential bugs, and other quality problems in your code, all without having to actually execute it. Based on these results, it also gives your code an overall quality score. Spyder's code analyzer is powered by the `Pylint <Pylint_>`__ backend, which can intelligently detect an enormous and customizable range of potential errors, bad practices, quality issues, style violations, and more."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/pylint.rst:13
msgid "Spyder Pylint pane, showing numerous issues discovered in a file"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/pylint.rst:22
msgid "Using the code analyzer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/pylint.rst:24
msgid "To select the desired file to analyze, make sure it is focused in the :ref:`panes-editor` by clicking anywhere within it. Then, the run the analysis, press the configurable shortcut (:kbd:`F8` by default), select :menuselection:`Source --> Run code analysis` from the menu bar or click the :guilabel:`Analyze` button in the Code Analysis pane. If the Code Analysis pane is not visible, you can open it under :menuselection:`View --> Panes --> Code Analysis`. All standard checks are run by default. Click any detected issue to go directly to the line with the failed check in the :ref:`panes-editor`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/pylint.rst:30
msgid "Spyder Pylint pane, showing running analysis and clicking failed check"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/pylint.rst:33
msgid "You can also manually enter the path of a file you'd like it to check in the path entry box in the pane's toolbar, which works with both individual scripts and whole Python packages (directories containing an :file:`__init__.py` file)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/pylint.rst:35
msgid "Spyder Pylint pane, showing running analysis browsing file"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/pylint.rst:38
msgid "Cancel analyzing a file with the :guilabel:`Stop` button, and in case the analysis were to fail, click the :guilabel:`Output` button to find out why. Otherwise, assuming Pylint succeeds, the :guilabel:`Output` will show the raw plain text analysis results on the selected file, allowing you to easily browse and copy/paste the full message names and descriptions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/pylint.rst:41
msgid "Spyder Pylint pane, showing output"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/pylint.rst:44
msgid "Finally, you can click the dropdown arrow in the filename field to view results of previous analyses."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/pylint.rst:46
msgid "Spyder Pylint pane, showing history"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/pylint.rst:57
msgid "The number of recent runs Spyder should remember can be customized in the :guilabel:`History...` dialog, available from the Code Analysis options (hamburger) menu in the top right of the pane."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/pylint.rst:59
msgid "Spyder Pylint pane, customizing history dialog"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/pylint.rst:62
msgid "You can also expand or collapse one or all the sections in the pane by using the corresponding options in that menu."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/pylint.rst:64
msgid "Spyder Pylint pane, expanding and collapsing sections"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/pylint.rst:73
msgid "Advanced options"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/pylint.rst:75
msgid "You can turn certain messages off at the line, block or file/module level by adding a ``# pylint: disable=MESSAGE-NAMES`` comment at the respective `scope <scope_>`__, where ``MESSAGE_NAMES`` should be replaced with a comma-separated list (or single value) of `Pylint message names <Pylint message names_>`__. For example, a directive might look like ``# pylint: disable=invalid-name``, or ``# pylint: disable=fixme, line-too-long``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/pylint.rst:82
msgid "Spyder Pylint pane, showing disabling a message locally"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/pylint.rst:85
msgid "Or, you can globally suppress specific messages and adjust other Pylint settings by editing the :file:`.pylintrc` configuration file in your user home folder. If it doesn't exist, you can generate it by running ``pylint --generate-rcfile > .pylintrc`` in your user home folder, from the Anaconda Prompt (on Windows) or your terminal (macOS/Linux). For more details on configuring Pylint, see the `Pylint configuration documentation <Pylint configuration documentation_>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/pylint.rst:91
msgid "Spyder Pylint pane, disabling a message globally in the .pylintrc"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/pylint.rst:103
msgid ":ref:`panes-profiler`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/remoteconnection.rst:5
msgid "Remote Connection Manager"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/remoteconnection.rst:7
msgid "The **Remote Connections Manager** allows you to create, initiate and manage connections to external servers as well as local containers and VMs for remote development and execution of your code. Connecting to a remote host and opening a new :ref:`panes-console` on it allows running code, browsing files and using other Spyder features just as if you were working on your local machine."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/remoteconnection.rst:10
msgid "Remote Connections Manager"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/remoteconnection.rst:13
msgid "It uses the standard, widely-used `SSH protocol <SSH protocol_>`__, which allows you to create a secure connection to remote servers, cloud resources and high-performance computing clusters, as well as local Docker containers, virtual machines (including Windows Subsystem for Linux v2), and headless devices such as the Raspberry Pi. Additionally, it supports connecting to `JupyterHub <JupyterHub_>`__ servers run by your company, institution or organization and using their shared Jupyter Server environments, without the limitations of the traditional notebook interface. No configuration is required on the remote host, aside from ensuring an SSH or JupyterHub server is running and accessible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/remoteconnection.rst:20
msgid "The remote connections manager can be accessed under :menuselection:`Tools --> Manage remote connections`, and you can use :menuselection:`Consoles --> New console in remote server` to open new consoles on remote servers you've already configured."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/remoteconnection.rst:22
msgid "Manage Remote Connections menu"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/remoteconnection.rst:30
msgid "Creating a new connection"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/remoteconnection.rst:32
msgid "To set up a connection to a new remote server, open the remote connections manager and click :guilabel:`New connection`. You can then configure the settings appropriate to the connection method."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/remoteconnection.rst:35
msgid "If your institution already has a `JupyterHub <JupyterHub_>`__ server you'd like to connect to, select the :guilabel:`JupyterHub` tab. Otherwise, if you want to connect to most other types of hosts, you'll want to use the default :guilabel:`SSH`. Either way, you'll just need to make sure JupyterHub or SSH is available on the remote machine, and you have the appropriate credentials to connect to it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/remoteconnection.rst:43
msgid "SSH connection"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/remoteconnection.rst:45
msgid "To create a new SSH connection, you need to enter several key details:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/remoteconnection.rst:47
msgid "Creating a new SSH connection"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/remoteconnection.rst:50
msgid "Select your authentication method, :guilabel:`Password` or :guilabel:`Key file`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/remoteconnection.rst:54
msgid ":guilabel:`Password` uses your normal username and password that you would enter when logging in to your account on the machine, while :guilabel:`Key file` uses a SSH private key you've generated with `ssh-keygen <ssh-keygen_>`__ and already registered with the remote machine. If you're unsure, try :guilabel:`Password` to start, or ask the person who set up the host you are trying to connect to."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/remoteconnection.rst:57
msgid "Type a :guilabel:`Name` for the connection, which is just used to identify it in within the Spyder interface."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/remoteconnection.rst:59
msgid "Enter the IP address or hostname of the device to connect to in :guilabel:`Remote address or host`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/remoteconnection.rst:61
msgid "If you or your administrator has configured different SSH port than the default ``22`` on the remote machine (the port the SSH daemon, ``sshd``, is listening on), enter it in the :guilabel:`Port` field."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/remoteconnection.rst:63
msgid "Type the :guilabel:`Username` of the account you want to connect to."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/remoteconnection.rst:65
msgid "If using a :guilabel:`Password`, enter it in the corresponding field. If using a :guilabel:`Key file`, navigate to where it is located (often under :file:`{YOUR_HOME_DIR}/.ssh/` and called :file:`id_rsa` or similar) and enter its passphrase, if you've set one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/remoteconnection.rst:67
msgid "Finally, you can optionally set the path to a `SSH configuration file <SSH configuration file_>`__, which can contain additional advanced options for the host you set above as well as filling in default values for the previous fields."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/remoteconnection.rst:74
msgid "Finally, click :guilabel:`Connect` to initiate the connection to the host, which will automatically validate and securely save all the provided details and autonomously set up the server Spyder needs to connect to on the remote host, so everything is ready for you to launch your first console."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/remoteconnection.rst:80
msgid "Alternatively, you can click :guilabel:`Save` to store the details you've entered in a new connection without actually trying to connect."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/remoteconnection.rst:86
msgid "JupyterHub connection"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/remoteconnection.rst:88
msgid "If connecting to a existing JupyterHub server, the process is much simpler than SSH. Just enter what you would like to :guilabel:`Name` the connection in the Spyder interface, the :guilabel:`Server URL` to the JupyterHub server to connect to, and the access :guilabel:`Token` you were provided. Then, click :guilabel:`Connect` to connect to the server and save the provided details for future use. If you have questions about how to obtain a token, ask the person who set up the JupyterHub server or your system administrator."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/remoteconnection.rst:95
msgid "To enable all of Spyder's features when working with an existing JupyterHub deployment, ask the person who set up the server to install the `Spyder-Remote-Services <Spyder-Remote-Services_>`__ Jupyter Server extension."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/remoteconnection.rst:99
msgid "Creating a new JupyterHub connection"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/remoteconnection.rst:107
msgid "Manage an existing connection"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/remoteconnection.rst:109
msgid "You can view the remote connections you've already created by browsing and selecting them from the left panel of the remote connections manager. The :guilabel:`Connection status` tab shows basic details of the connection, its current state, a log of connection events and any errors to help troubleshoot problems. Under the :guilabel:`Connection info` tab, you can update any of the details you entered when :ref:`panes-remote-create`, using the :guilabel:`Save` button at the bottom to save your changes. Use the :guilabel:`Remove` button to delete the selected connection."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/remoteconnection.rst:124
msgid "Connecting to existing kernels (advanced)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/remoteconnection.rst:128
msgid "This is an advanced feature for connecting to existing running kernels, which is substantially more complicated and less capable than creating and managing a connection with Spyder using the Remote Connections Manager. If possible, we recommend using the latter instead unless your use case requires it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/remoteconnection.rst:131
msgid "You can connect to external local and remote kernels (including those managed by Jupyter Notebook or QtConsole) through the :guilabel:`Connect to an existing kernel` dialog under the :guilabel:`Consoles` menu. For this feature to work, a compatible version of the ``spyder-kernels`` package :ref:`must be installed <troubleshooting-common-kernel-version>` in the environment or machine in which the external kernel is running."
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/remoteconnection.rst:134
msgid "Connect to existing kernels dialog"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/remoteconnection.rst:142
msgid "Connect to a local kernel"
msgstr ""

#: ../../panes/remoteconnection.rst:144
msgid "To connect to a local kernel that is already running (e.g. one started by Jupyter notebook),"
